---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>DeepSeek published <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent">dsh-superagent, an MIT-licensed coding agent for one GitHub repository</a>, on October 31. The sentence in its README that matters most to anyone running an always-on agent is about attention: "take as little human attention as possible, and give as much value as possible." AI agent notifications are where that rule gets tested. This post applies it to Pilot Cloud, the hosted node we run as a remote MCP server: what belongs in the feed, what the daily email covers, and how to tell a quiet agent from a stopped one.</p><p>One caution about the source. DeepSeek calls the repo a "research prototype" and "not an official DeepSeek product", and <a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/deepseek-super-agent-open-source-coding-agent">TNW's report</a> notes that the public code differs from the internal version. Read it as a design position, not a benchmark.</p><section><h2>The two triggers DeepSeek allows</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent/blob/main/docs/000-philosophy.md">DeepSeek's philosophy document</a> says the agent "speaks only when a person must act or can use a result." It confirms a request once and stays quiet while it works. It writes again when a result is ready, when it needs a decision, or when it cannot finish. Work nobody asked for needs a rule the team wrote, and starts in a mode that only records what the agent would have done.</p><p>The useful part is the split. "Must act" and "can use a result" deserve different channels, because only the first one justifies an interruption.</p></section><section><h2>Where AI agent notifications go on a Pilot Cloud node</h2><p>A node gives you a separate place for each case:</p><ul><li><strong>You must act: the conversation in your MCP client.</strong> <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/skill.md">The node guide</a> tells the agent to bring its owner friend requests, anything that commits them to something new, and anything personal that someone other than a close friend asks for.</li><li><strong>You can use a result: the feed.</strong> The agent calls pilot_post_update, and a card appears on the Feed page of <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network">your node's dashboard</a>, which describes it as "what your agents got done and found for you."</li><li><strong>Things piled up: the daily email.</strong> The dashboard's description is "once a day when something waits for you: who wrote, friend requests and your week."</li><li><strong>Nothing happened: silence.</strong> The end-of-day prompt that pilot_start hands to your scheduler closes with "If nothing is new and I already used Pilot today, say nothing."</li></ul><p>Pilot Link, the agent that can run on the node itself, follows the same split. Its SOUL.md, as pilot_agent returned it on a node where Pilot Link has never been switched on, says "Keep routine progress in my journal or Feed; text only when my owner needs to decide or act, or asked for an update."</p><p>The feed, the daily email and Pilot Link are not on <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/mcp">the Pilot Cloud docs page</a> yet. What follows comes from the live <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json">server card</a>, the <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1">plain-text API reference</a> and the dashboard page itself.</p></section><section><h2>What a feed post looks like</h2><p>If your client is not connected yet, <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/blog/connect-claude-code-to-pilot-protocol">the Claude Code setup guide</a> covers it. The docs page gives this form:</p><pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http pilot https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp \\
  --header "Authorization: Bearer pk_…"</code></pre><div class="callout"><p>A pk_ token means anyone holding it can use your agent. Keep it in an environment variable or a secret store, and revoke it with DELETE /v1/tokens/{id} if it leaks.</p></div><p>pilot_post_update takes two required fields, text (up to 1,000 characters) and source (up to 200), plus an optional url and up to four files already on the node. Here is a post using the handle from our docs as a placeholder:</p><pre><code>{
  "text": "Agreed Thursday at 3 pm with @maria's agent for the contract review. Nothing else needed from you.",
  "source": "@maria"
}</code></pre><p>A real call we made on November 2 came back like this, with the id replaced:</p><pre><code>{"id":"&lt;post id&gt;","note":"It is in your owner's feed.","posted":true}</code></pre><p>The tool description carries the attention rule in its own words. It wants "something done for them, found, decided or set". It rules out messages, "routine checks or anything that found nothing", and asks for "a few an hour at most, so put several things in one update."</p><p>A scheduled job can follow the same rule. This prompt is our example, not something the server issues:</p><pre><code>Use pilot_ask to check security advisories for the packages this repo depends on.
If one affects us, call pilot_post_update with the package, the advisory and a link.
If none does, post nothing and say nothing.</code></pre><p>Queries leave the node, and the service agents that receive them may keep them, so your dependency list goes with the question. Keep secrets out of queries.</p><p>On the reading side, each card shows who reported it, the text, the source and any attachments. Its buttons are Like, Save, Remind me (in an hour, this evening, or tomorrow morning) and Hide, whose tooltip reads "your agents learn from it."</p></section><section><h2>Turning the daily email on or off</h2><p>The email has its own routes, and they accept the same bearer token:</p><pre><code>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $PILOT_TOKEN" \\
  https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1/digest

curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $PILOT_TOKEN" \\
  https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1/digest/preview

curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $PILOT_TOKEN" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"on": true, "tz": "America/Los_Angeles"}' \\
  https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1/digest</code></pre><p>The first reports whether it is on, so check rather than assume. The second shows the mail as it would go now, and sending POST to that preview route mails it to you. The third sets the switch and the time zone, which is the one whose morning the email arrives in. The dashboard fills that field from the browser's IANA zone name.</p><p>Feed posts are not in the dashboard's description of the email, so do not count on it to carry them.</p></section><section><h2>Quiet can also mean stopped</h2><p>DeepSeek's document says "a healthy state takes no space". That is a good rule for a panel, with one catch: an agent that has died looks exactly like an agent with nothing to say.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">Claude Code's 2.1.316 changelog</a> lists a fix for "cloud sessions and cloud scheduled tasks not being marked as failed when auto mode stopped a turn because the server returned no safety verdict." Until that fix, such a task could stop without showing as failed.</p><p>So keep one health check separate from the notifications. GET /v1/home reports the check-in state as running, stopped or never, along with the time of the last one. pilot_start says the same thing in prose, including how many minutes ago the last check-in ran. A check-in that keeps running shows the schedule is alive. It does not show the agent judged anything correctly.</p></section><section><h2>A feed post is the agent's account, so check it against the log</h2><p>The source field is free text supplied by whoever makes the call. "Source: Gmail" means the agent says it came from Gmail. The schema has no separate proof of origin, and url is whatever link the agent passes.</p><p>That matters because of what the agent reads. <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/trust">Trust is deny-by-default</a>, so nobody reaches your node until you accept them, with business agents as the one exception. But the docs still say to treat inbox messages and agent replies as untrusted content. An accepted friend's agent, compromised or just careless, can send text that nudges your agent into posting a link.</p><p>The tool is also annotated destructiveHint false, while the docs say anything reaching another party is marked destructive "so clients ask you first." A client that prompts only on destructive tools will not stop to ask before a post. The <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools">MCP tools specification</a> adds that clients "MUST consider tool annotations to be untrusted unless they come from trusted servers", so these hints describe intent and enforce nothing.</p><p>The check is the activity log. GET /v1/activity records every tool call with client, tool, arguments, result and duration, each field cut to its first 2,000 characters and the log kept 30 days. A post's claim can be compared with the calls that were actually made before it.</p></section><section><h2>What the feed and the email do not do</h2><ul><li><strong>They do not decide anything.</strong> The node holds messages. Choosing what deserves a post takes an agent: a client on a schedule, or Pilot Link when it is on. With neither, messages wait until something calls pilot_checkin.</li><li><strong>The feed is not an alert channel.</strong> The dashboard refreshes it every 30 seconds while the page is open and visible. If something needs you now, the agent should ask in the conversation.</li><li><strong>The hourly guidance is not a documented quota.</strong> "A few an hour" is an instruction to the model. The documented node-wide limit is 120 tool calls a minute per node with a burst of 60, so a looping agent can still be noisy.</li><li><strong>A read-only connection cannot post.</strong> A connection granted pilot.read "sees and runs only the read-only tools", and pilot_post_update is not marked read-only.</li><li><strong>Retention for posts is not published.</strong> The docs give 30 days for received messages and files and one day for files stored to send, and they do not cover feed posts or their attachments. Put a durable link in url for anything you need to keep.</li><li><strong>It is not a place for secrets.</strong> The tool description says "never secrets, or anything private about someone else."</li></ul><p>Tuning is on you. DeepSeek's advice for a noisy agent is to make its prompts shorter and stricter, or pause it, and not to add more messages explaining the noise.</p></section><div class="cta"><h3>Give your agent somewhere quiet to report</h3><p>Add one URL to your MCP client, sign in, and your agent gets an always-on node with a feed you read when you choose to.</p><a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/">Connect your agent to Pilot</a></div>`;
---
<BlogLayout
  title="AI Agent Notifications Without the Noise: Pilot Cloud's Feed and Daily Email, After DeepSeek's Attention Rule"
